<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why This Proxy is Sound.</h5>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Our choice to manipulate <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is justified because its impact on the final task vector is well-behaved and controllable. The error introduced by sparsifying <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="Sx3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\Delta W-\Delta W^{\prime}(\bm{\alpha})\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\Delta W-\Delta W^{\prime}(\bm{\alpha})\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|B\cdot A-B\cdot\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|B\cdot A-B\cdot\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\|B\|_{F}\cdot\|A-\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\|B\|_{F}\cdot\|A-\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This inequality is the core of our formal argument. It demonstrates that the total approximation error in the task vector <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> is directly controlled by how well the sparsified matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> approximates the original matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. By performing magnitude-based pruning on <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we are directly minimizing the term <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A-\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A-\mathbf{S}_{\bm{\alpha}}(A)\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn constrains the overall error. This provides a stable, principled mechanism for denoising.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we connect our practical objective (minimizing validation loss) to the ideal one (getting close to <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) with the following standard machine learning assumptions:</p>
<ul id="Sx3.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="Sx3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimality of Ideal Solution:</span> The ideal vector <math id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is, by definition, the minimizer of the true, unobserved loss: <math id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{*}=\underset{\Delta W^{\prime}}{\text{argmin}}\mathcal{L}(\Delta W^{\prime},\mathcal{D}_{\text{true}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>argmin</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{*}=\underset{\Delta W^{\prime}}{\text{argmin}}\mathcal{L}(\Delta W^{\prime},\mathcal{D}_{\text{true}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proxy Quality:</span> The validation loss is a smooth and faithful estimator of the true loss.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This creates a logical chain: minimizing the validation loss serves as a proxy for minimizing the true loss, which ideally leads to a solution close to the optimal sparse vector <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use a derivative-free optimizer (CMA-ES) to find a near-optimal <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical Validation.</h5>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The conceptual hypothesis and formal justification lead to a clear, testable prediction: If matrix A is indeed the primary locus of learnable concepts and noise, then sparsifying it should be more effective and safer than sparsifying matrix B.
We test this by comparing our proposed asymmetric approach (Sparsifying A) against the alternative (Sparsifying B). Our empirical results in Figures <a href="#Sx3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Formal Justification. ‣ Theoretical and Empirical Analysis ‣ Our Methodology ‣ Black-box Model Merging for Language-Model-as-a-Service with Massive Model Repositories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Sx3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Formal Justification. ‣ Theoretical and Empirical Analysis ‣ Our Methodology ‣ Black-box Model Merging for Language-Model-as-a-Service with Massive Model Repositories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> confirm our prediction:
1) It results in a final task vector <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> with a higher Gini coefficient, indicating a sparser and more concentrated representation of knowledge;
2) The “Sparsifying A” strategy consistently retains higher performance on downstream tasks after merging.
This provides strong empirical evidence that matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contains a higher density of both task-specific knowledge and noise, making it the right target for sparsification.
This finding aligns with the conclusions from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benedek and Wolf 2024</a>)</cite> which also examined that pruning matrix A is a safer and more effective strategy than targeting matrix B.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section evaluates our proposed Black-Box Merging (<span id="Sx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BMM</span>) framework, <span id="Sx4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Evo-Merging</span>. We benchmark it against a suite of baselines and perform in-depth ablation studies to validate our design choices. Evo-Merging ’s implementation details are in appendix  <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Evo-Merging Implementation Details ‣ Black-box Model Merging for Language-Model-as-a-Service with Massive Model Repositories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and  <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Algorithm flow of Evo-Merging ‣ Black-box Model Merging for Language-Model-as-a-Service with Massive Model Repositories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experimental Setups</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Datasets and Metrics.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate our model’s efficacy, we evaluate its performance on large-scale in-domain and out-of-domain tasks. Our framework uses the Llama 3.1 Instruct model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">The Llama 3.1 Team 2024</a>)</cite> and the InstructUIE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2023</a>)</cite>—chosen for its generative complexity—to fine-tune over 100 models on information extraction tasks (e.g., NER, RE, and EE). We evaluate all methods using Macro Precision (Prec) and Macro F1-Score (F1). Further details on the datasets and metrics are in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Dataset Details and Metric ‣ Black-box Model Merging for Language-Model-as-a-Service with Massive Model Repositories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
