<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Evo-Merging Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Environment.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the model merging stage, we utilized an Intel(R) Xeon(R) Platinum 8352Y CPU @ 2.20GHz. For the inference stage, the resulting models were deployed on GPUs: an NVIDIA A800 (80 GB VRAM).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Core Optimization Parameters.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Evo-Merging</span> framework is built on the Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hansen and Ostermeier 1996</a>)</cite>, for which we used the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cma</span> Python package. Across all experiments, we used a fixed LoRa rank of 8, a CMA-ES population size of 20, a sigma of 0.05, a bound of 1.5, and an L1 regularization coefficient (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) of 0.05.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training and Inference details.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct our repository of over 100 specialized models, we performed fine-tuning on the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama 3.1 8B Instruct</span> model for each downstream task. We applied the <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AdamW optimizer</span> throughout all training processes. The training was configured with a batch size of 128, and a maximum sequence length (<span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cutoff_len</span>) of 2048 tokens. All models were trained for 20 epochs (<span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">num_train_epochs</span>) with an initial learning rate of 1e-4. We employed a cosine learning rate scheduler (<span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lr_scheduler_type</span>) with a warm-up ratio (<span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">warmup_ratio</span>) of 0.1, where the learning rate was gradually increased during the initial 10% of training steps before following the cosine decay.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the generation process during inference, we utilized a nucleus sampling strategy with a <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">temperature</span> of 0.95, <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">top_p</span> of 0.7, and <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">top_k</span> of 50. The maximum number of newly generated tokens (<span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_new_tokens</span>) was set to 1024.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Setups and Checkpointing.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The specific configuration for our two primary experimental settings is summarized in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Experimental Setups and Checkpointing. ‣ Appendix A Evo-Merging Implementation Details ‣ Black-box Model Merging for Language-Model-as-a-Service with Massive Model Repositories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We tailored the number of iterations to the complexity of each task. To mitigate overfitting, the loss was accumulated over the entire validation set for each fitness evaluation, and we employed a greedy checkpointing strategy, saving the best model found during the search. The final models reported in this paper correspond to the iteration number listed in the table.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
